DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmppaatt produces a PPM of the specified width and height, with a pattern
       in it.

       You could, for example, use it  to  create  wallpaper  for  a  computer
       screen.

       One  use  of  this program is as an example of the Netpbm library ddrraaww‐‐
       iinngg(1) functions, which it uses.

       Some of the patterns have large numbers of colors, so  if  you  want  a
       simpler pattern, use ppnnmmqquuaanntt on the output.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ),  ppppmmppaatt  recognizes  the
       following command line options:


   PPaatttteerrnn SSppeecciiffiiccaattiioonn
       Specify  the pattern type with these options.  One pattern type must be
       specified.



       --ggiinngghhaamm22 --gg22
              A gingham check pattern.  Can be tiled.

              If you specify -color, give two  colors:  background  and  fore‐
              ground, in that order.


       --ggiinngghhaamm33 --gg33
              A slightly more complicated gingham.  Can be tiled.

              If  you  specify  -color,  give three colors: background and two
              foregrounds, in that order.


       --mmaaddrraass
              A madras plaid.  Can be tiled.

              If you specify -color, give three  colors:  background  and  two
              foregrounds, in that order.


       --ttaarrttaann
              A tartan plaid.  Can be tiled.

              If  you  specify  -color,  give three colors: background and two
              foregrounds, in that order.


       --ppoolleess Color gradients centered on randomly-placed poles.

              If you specify -color, give two or more colors.


       --ssqquuiigg Squiggley tubular pattern.  Can be tiled.

              If you specify -color, give three or more colors.  The first  is
              the background color.


       --ccaammoo  Camouflage pattern.

              If  you specify --ccoolloorr, give three or more colors.  The first is
              the background color; the others are colors for the leafy  fore‐
              ground  shapes.   The  foreground  shapes  will  probably occupy
              nearly the entire image, so that the background color is  barely
              visible.


       --aannttiiccaammoo
              Anti-camouflage pattern - like -camo, but ultra-bright colors.

              If you specify --ccoolloorr, this is the same as --ccaammoo.


       --aarrggyyllee11
              A  diamond  argyle pattern, without a cross through the diamond,
              with one diamond.  Can be tiled.

              If you specify -color, give two  colors:  background  and  fore‐
              ground, in that order.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.78 (March 2017).


       --aarrggyyllee22
              A diamond argyle pattern, with a cross through the diamond, with
              one diamond.  Can be tiled.

              If you specify -color,  give  three  colors:  background,  fore‐
              ground, and stripe, in that order.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.78 (March 2017).




   OOtthheerr OOppttiioonnss
       --ccoolloorr _c_o_l_o_r_l_i_s_t
              This specifies the colors to appear in the pattern.

              If you do not specify this option, ppppmmppaatt chooses colors at ran‐
              dom.

              Different patterns take different numbers of colors.   Some  can
              involve  variable numbers of colors.  If you specify a number of
              colors incompatible with the pattern you specify, ppppmmppaatt  fails,
              telling you how many colors to specify.

              _c_o_l_o_r_l_i_s_t is a comma-separated list of colors.

              Specify  each  color  as  described  for  the  argument  of  the
              ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(())                 library                 routine
              ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

              Example: --ccoolloorr rreedd,, ggrreeeenn,, rrggbbii::11..00//00..55//..2255 .

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.78 (March 2017).


       --mmeesshh  This  option  affects  the  patterns generated by the --ggiinngghhaamm22,
              --ggiinngghhaamm33, --mmaaddrraass, and --ttaarrttaann.  When this option is _n_o_t speci‐
              fied, when two colors intersect, the program mixes them and puts
              the average in the rectangular intersection region.  with --mmeesshh,
              the  program  fills that region with a checkerboard pattern con‐
              sisting of the two colors.  The resulting  image  looks  like  a
              true  woven  fabric, with separate threads for the separate col‐
              ors.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.97 (December 2021).



       --rraannddoommsseeeedd _i_n_t_e_g_e_r
              This is the seed for the random number generator that  generates
              the pixels.

              Use  this  to  ensure you get the same image on separate invoca‐
              tions.

              By default, ppppmmppaatt uses a seed derived from the time of day  and
              process  ID, which gives you fairly uncorrelated results in mul‐
              tiple invocations.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.61 (December 2012).




